  "answer": "*Attention Is All You Need* is not indexed in the local paper corpus; the following summary is derived from web sources retrieved on 2026-10-27 [W1, W2].\n\n* **Overview and Authorship**: Authored by Ashish Vaswani et al. (affiliated with Google Brain, Google Research, and the University of Toronto), the paper was published at NeurIPS 2017 (as a spotlight) under arXiv identifier 1706.03762 [W1, W2, W3].\n* **Core Architecture**: The paper introduces the Transformer architecture, which completely eliminates recurrence (RNNs) and convolutions in favor of stacked self-attention mechanisms and positional encodings, overcoming the sequential computation bottlenecks that limit GPU throughput [W1, W3].\n* **Benchmark Performance**: \n  * On the WMT 2014 English-to-German translation benchmark, the Transformer achieved a state-of-the-art BLEU score of 28.4, outperforming prior models (including ensembles) by over 2.0 BLEU [W5, W6, W7].\n  * On the WMT 2014 English-to-French benchmark, it established a single-model state-of-the-art BLEU score of 41.8 (with 41.0 reported for the big model variant) after 3.5 days of training on eight GPUs, requiring a fraction of the training cost of earlier models [W5, W6, W7].\n* **Limitations and Impact**: Transformers are noted to be data-hungry, requiring massive training sets (such as the 36-million-sentence WMT 2014 English-French dataset) and risking overfitting on smaller datasets [W5]. Despite this, the architecture has become the primary foundation for modern artificial intelligence systems, including large language models [W1, W3]."
